  "coverage": [
    "All mutable and const public transcript mutations reject unowned outer transactions before SQL writes or snapshot publication. Forty format/outcome/operation combinations cover both outer commit and rollback; mixed checkpoint/ordinary rows, clean copied snapshots and next owning writes are verified.",
    "Provenance records immutable committed mappings and pending read-only normalizations, not speculative nested revisions or tombstones. Owning failures preserve snapshots. Allocation-fault cases cover append, revision and replacement; deferred commit and retry-before-reload regressions remain passing.",
    "Legacy blob writes verify the exact original blob and operation boundary. Truncated/stale append, length mismatch and edits outside requested revision positions reject conversion. Valid append/revision/replacement materialize canonical rows and references transactionally.",
    "Converted legacy snapshots verify untouched row content and a global preparation version before writing; a successful mutable handoff returns to suffix-only appends. Const legacy views remain verified until refreshed. Copied blob projections retain the original read-only normalization across another snapshot's conversion, so a different committed checkpoint remains a conflict. Explicit const replacement may repeat an already completed removal; stale append and revision cannot restore the removed row.",
    "Default repaired loads capture transcript, provider rows/revision and required journal inputs coherently before filesystem projection. An external coherent transcript/journal commit during projection does not mix snapshots. Optional token failure after SQLite rollback uses already captured inputs, clears trust and issues zero subsequent SQL reads.",
    "Persistent reconciliation checks database-wide data version, same-connection total changes and schema version with exactly two preparation attempts. Unrelated commits and same-connection DML/DDL cover successful retry and second invalidation without destination or reconciliation mutation.",
    "Newly reconstructed journal outcomes use the same prepared canonical payload as transcript rows, preserving message IDs and portable checkpoint references. Existing completed full results, preview projections, read_file user JSON, recovery uncertainty, relocation, forks and no automatic historical replay remain covered.",
    "Const adapters and reconciliation borrow provider state. Allocation instrumentation includes a 9 MiB provider history and a separate 9 MiB unreferenced historical journal result; ordinary append/revision/reconciliation avoid copying either. Required journal reads use indexed current-transcript call IDs.",
    "Single-message normalization and position-sensitive ID generation are shared. Pending normalization merges with explicit revision into one update; ordinary row appends serialize changed suffixes only and avoid acknowledged-row reads and retained-payload copying.",
    "Retirement workspace is limited to replaced JSON and owned temporary messages. Nested JSON and post-commit allocation faults verify both mutable and const publication. The reported overflow remains unconfirmed; no security remediation or broad JSON-lifetime redesign is claimed.",
    "Backup diagnostics distinguish verified retained files from publication-unconfirmed candidate locations through the configured filesystem. Failures before publication, after publication and during readback retain the original diagnostic; successful backup/backups report fields remain compatible.",
    "Twenty shuffled repetitions of all twenty-one fault cases, the full host/sanitizer/Linux matrices, three static analyzers, analyzer runner/detection tests, pinned Semgrep fixtures/production, all persistence/capture benchmarks and Tiger cross-build/static checks passed.",
    "Schema 7, strict archive trust, lazy anchor pinning, provider interfaces, tool schemas, effort defaults, CLI flags, both serialized cache limits, capture quotas and database-wide repair invalidation with one retry remain unchanged."
  ],
